Q：你都见过哪些神级评论？
@落叶知秋 ：前段时候点外卖看到的，笑了好长时间


Q：为什么在那些允许「作恶」的高自由度RPG里，90%的玩家最后还是老老实实当了「大善人」？ 
@只是个过路的 ：现实中做好人还有这样那样的考虑，游戏里我可以尽情当好人。
Q： 有没有可以笑上一年的笑话？
@深港双城男 ：能喝酒就不要喝水，把水留给不会喝酒的孩子。


Q： 反直觉的地理知识有哪些？
@转轮高手嫪毐 ：漠河到大连，比大连到台北还要远 100 来公里。
华沙一样远。
柏林到莫斯科，和喀什到哈密一样远。小胡子辛辛苦苦闪击苏联，搁中国还没打出新疆。
Q：为什么炸鸡，炸猪都很常见，但很少听说炸牛？
@wgj0227 ：这样，您买张高铁票去天津，随手找个清真馆子，点一份芝麻里脊

Some Maya vases depict dogs walking below a ruler's hammock, which has been interpreted&nbsp;as the dog escorting the ruler's burial procession into the afterlife. It's just one role that&nbsp;dogs may have played in ancient&nbsp;Maya culture.&nbsp;   
Depictions of dogs in Maya art and archaeological finds point to a variety of possible roles in the ancient culture.&nbsp;

Long before the advent of kennel clubs, backyard breeders, and animal rescues, the ancient Maya traded dogs over long distances. 

“Dogs are the oldest domesticated animal worldwide,” says Elizabeth Paris, an archaeologist specializing in the ancient Maya at the University of Calgary and a National Geographic Explorer. “The Maya valued those relationships and honestly, went through a lot of time and trouble to g…
